-- title:
--   Section 2, (2.12)–(2.13) — the Skorohod $M_1$ topology on $D((0,\infty),\mathbb R^d)$, u.o.c. convergence, and weak convergence in coupling form
-- statement:
--   This file fixes the modes of convergence used in Section 4 of Chen and Whitt (1993). A path is a function $x:\mathbb R\to E$ with values in a real normed space $E$ (in the paper $E=\mathbb R^d$); only its values on the relevant time interval matter.
--
--   1. **Completed graph (2.12).** For $a<b$, the completed graph of $x$ on $[a,b]$ is
--   $$
--   \Gamma_x=\{(t,z): t\in[a,b],\ z=\alpha\,x(t-)+(1-\alpha)\,x(t)\text{ for some }\alpha\in[0,1]\},
--   $$
--   where $x(t-)$ is the left limit, with the convention $x(a-)=x(a)$ at the left endpoint. At a jump the graph contains the whole straight segment from $x(t-)$ to $x(t)$ — one segment in $E$, not one per coordinate.
--   2. **Order and parametric representations.** $\Gamma_x$ is ordered by $(t_1,z_1)\le(t_2,z_2)$ if $t_1<t_2$, or $t_1=t_2$ and $\|z_1-x(t_1-)\|\le\|z_2-x(t_1-)\|$. A parametric representation of $\Gamma_x$ is a continuous map $s\mapsto(\tilde t(s),\tilde x(s))$ from $[0,1]$ onto $\Gamma_x$ that is nondecreasing for this order.
--   3. **$M_1$ convergence on $[a,b]$ (2.13).** $x_n\to x$ in $M_1$ on $[a,b]$ when for every $\varepsilon>0$, for all large $n$, there are parametric representations $(\tilde t_n,\tilde x_n)$ of $\Gamma_{x_n}$ and $(\tilde t,\tilde x)$ of $\Gamma_x$ with
--   $$
--   \sup_{0\le s\le 1}\|\tilde x_n(s)-\tilde x(s)\|<\varepsilon,\qquad \sup_{0\le s\le 1}|\tilde t_n(s)-\tilde t(s)|<\varepsilon,
--   $$
--   i.e. $d_{M_1}(x_n|_{[a,b]},x|_{[a,b]})\to 0$.
--   4. **$M_1$ convergence on $(0,\infty)$.** $x_n\to x$ in $D((0,\infty),E,M_1)$ when all the paths $x_n$ and $x$ lie in $D((0,\infty),E)$ (right-continuous with left limits at every $t>0$) and $x_n\to x$ in $M_1$ on $[a,b]$ for all $0<a<b$ such that $x$ is continuous at $a$ and at $b$.
--   5. **u.o.c. convergence.** $x_n\to x$ uniformly on compact subsets of $[0,\infty)$: $\sup_{0\le t\le T}\|x_n(t)-x(t)\|\to 0$ for every $T$.
--   6. **Weak convergence in coupling form.** Random elements $X_n$ (on $(\Omega,\mathbb P)$) and $X'$ (on $(\Omega',\mathbb P')$) of a measurable space $E$ satisfy $X_n\Rightarrow X'$ for a mode of convergence $c$ when there is a probability space carrying random elements $Y_n,Y'$ with $\mathrm{Law}(Y_n)=\mathrm{Law}(X_n)$ for all large $n$, $\mathrm{Law}(Y')=\mathrm{Law}(X')$, and $c(Y_n(\omega),Y'(\omega))$ for almost every $\omega$.
--
--   These are the only notions of convergence that the mission's theorems use: $M_1$ on $(0,\infty)$ for the conclusions, u.o.c. for the primitive processes, whose limits are continuous.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The page requires only that $\tilde t$ be nondecreasing. The standard $M_1$ topology of Skorohod, Pomarede and Whitt, used here, also requires monotone motion along each jump segment. Without that, a path oscillating near a jump could converge to a single jump, which gives a strictly weaker topology. The page defines $M_1$ only on $D([0,T],\mathbb R^n)$. On $(0,\infty)$ the standard characterization via restrictions to $[a,b]$ at continuity points $0<a<b$ of the limit is used (Whitt 1980, Section 2), and the paper cites it for this purpose. For the Polish spaces of the paper (with the path σ-algebra generated by evaluations), convergence in the coupling form is equivalent to weak convergence, by the Skorohod representation theorem and bounded convergence. The laws are taken on the σ-algebra generated by evaluations, which sees only finite-dimensional distributions; this is why convergence in $D((0,\infty),E,M_1)$ includes the requirement that the paths be càdlàg. Without it, a copy of the limit modified at a random countable dense set of times would have the same law and no continuity points, and the $M_1$ condition would hold vacuously.
-- source:
--   Chen and Whitt, Diffusion approximations for open queueing networks with service interruptions, Queueing Systems 13 (1993), p. 341, Eqs. (2.12)–(2.13) ("For x ∈ D([0, T], ℝⁿ), the completed graph of x is Γₓ = {(s, t) : s = αx(t−) + (1 − α)x(t) for some α, 0 ≤ α ≤ 1}. (2.12) A parametric representation of Γₓ is a continuous function (x̃, t̃) mapping [0, 1] onto Γₓ such that t̃(·) is nondecreasing. […] d_{M₁}(x₁, x₂) = inf {‖x̃₁ − x̃₂‖ ∨ ‖t̃₁ − t̃₂‖} (2.13)"); p. 336 and Whitt [30], Section 2, for D((0, ∞)); p. 351 for u.o.c. and the Skorohod representation

import Mathlib

namespace ChenWhitt93.JumpDiffusion

open Filter Topology MeasureTheory

/-!
Chen and Whitt (1993), Section 2, pp. 341–342: the Skorohod M₁ topology, and the two modes of
convergence used in Section 4 (uniform convergence on compacts, and weak convergence of random
paths written in coupling form).

Paths are functions `x : ℝ → E` into a real normed space `E` (in the paper `E = ℝⁿ`); only the
values on the time interval under consideration matter.
-/

/-- The left limit of `x` at `t`, computed for the restriction of `x` to `[a, ∞)`: at the left
endpoint `t = a` it is `x a` (the convention `x(a−) = x(a)` of `D([a, b], ℝⁿ)`), otherwise it is
`Function.leftLim x t` (which is `x t` when the left limit does not exist). -/
noncomputable def leftLimFrom {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] (a : ℝ) (x : ℝ → E) (t : ℝ) : E :=
  if t = a then x a else Function.leftLim x t

/-- Eq. (2.12): the completed graph of the restriction of `x` to `[a, b]`, the set of pairs
`(t, z)` with `t ∈ [a, b]` and `z = α x(t−) + (1 − α) x(t)` for some `α ∈ [0, 1]` (the pair is
written (time, space) here, (space, time) on the page). -/
def completedGraph {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [NormedSpace ℝ E]
    (a b : ℝ) (x : ℝ → E) : Set (ℝ × E) :=
  {p | p.1 ∈ Set.Icc a b ∧
    ∃ α ∈ Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) 1, p.2 = α • leftLimFrom a x p.1 + (1 - α) • x p.1}

/-- The order of the completed graph (Skorohod; Whitt): `(t₁, z₁) ≤ (t₂, z₂)` if `t₁ < t₂`, or
`t₁ = t₂` and `z₁` is at most as far from `x(t₁−)` as `z₂` along the jump segment. -/
def graphLE {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] (a : ℝ) (x : ℝ → E) (p q : ℝ × E) : Prop :=
  p.1 < q.1 ∨ (p.1 = q.1 ∧ ‖p.2 - leftLimFrom a x p.1‖ ≤ ‖q.2 - leftLimFrom a x q.1‖)

/-- A parametric representation of the completed graph of `x` on `[a, b]`: a continuous map
`s ↦ (r s, u s)` from `[0, 1]` onto the completed graph which is nondecreasing for the order
`graphLE` (so the time component `r` is nondecreasing, and along each jump segment the spatial
component moves monotonically from `x(t−)` to `x(t)`). -/
def IsParamRep {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [NormedSpace ℝ E]
    (a b : ℝ) (x : ℝ → E) (r : ℝ → ℝ) (u : ℝ → E) : Prop :=
  ContinuousOn r (Set.Icc 0 1) ∧ ContinuousOn u (Set.Icc 0 1) ∧
  (fun s => (r s, u s)) '' Set.Icc 0 1 = completedGraph a b x ∧
  ∀ s₁ ∈ Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) 1, ∀ s₂ ∈ Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) 1, s₁ ≤ s₂ →
    graphLE a x (r s₁, u s₁) (r s₂, u s₂)

/-- Eq. (2.13): `d_{M₁}(xₙ|[a,b], x|[a,b]) → 0`, written without the metric: for every `ε > 0`,
eventually there are parametric representations `(r', u')` of `xₙ` and `(r, u)` of `x` on
`[a, b]` that are uniformly `ε`-close in both components. -/
def M1TendstoOn {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [NormedSpace ℝ E]
    (a b : ℝ) (xs : ℕ → ℝ → E) (x : ℝ → E) : Prop :=
  ∀ ε : ℝ, 0 < ε → ∀ᶠ n in atTop, ∃ (r' : ℝ → ℝ) (u' : ℝ → E) (r : ℝ → ℝ) (u : ℝ → E),
    IsParamRep a b (xs n) r' u' ∧ IsParamRep a b x r u ∧
    ∀ s ∈ Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) 1, ‖u' s - u s‖ < ε ∧ |r' s - r s| < ε

/-- `x` is an element of `D((0, ∞), E)`: at every `t > 0` it is right-continuous and has a left
limit. -/
def IsCadlagPos {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] (x : ℝ → E) : Prop :=
  ∀ t : ℝ, 0 < t → ContinuousWithinAt x (Set.Ici t) t ∧ ∃ l : E, Tendsto x (𝓝[<] t) (𝓝 l)

/-- Convergence `xₙ → x` in `D((0, ∞), E, M₁)`: all paths lie in `D((0, ∞), E)`, and
`xₙ|[a,b] → x|[a,b]` in `M₁` for all `0 < a < b` with `a` and `b` continuity points of `x`
(Whitt 1980, Section 2). -/
def M1Tendsto {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [NormedSpace ℝ E]
    (xs : ℕ → ℝ → E) (x : ℝ → E) : Prop :=
  (∀ n, IsCadlagPos (xs n)) ∧ IsCadlagPos x ∧
  ∀ a b : ℝ, 0 < a → a < b → ContinuousAt x a → ContinuousAt x b → M1TendstoOn a b xs x

/-- Uniform convergence on compact subintervals of `[0, ∞)` (u.o.c.). -/
def UocTendsto {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] (xs : ℕ → ℝ → E) (x : ℝ → E) : Prop :=
  ∀ T : ℝ, ∀ ε : ℝ, 0 < ε → ∀ᶠ n in atTop, ∀ t ∈ Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) T, ‖xs n t - x t‖ < ε

/-- Weak convergence `Xₙ ⇒ X'` of random elements of `E`, in coupling (Skorohod representation)
form, for a mode of convergence `conv` of deterministic sequences in `E`: there is a probability
space carrying random elements `Yₙ`, `Y'` of `E` such that `Yₙ` has the law of `Xₙ` (for all
large `n`), `Y'` has the law of `X'`, and `conv (Yₙ(ω)) (Y'(ω))` for almost every `ω`. Laws are
image measures on `E`'s σ-algebra (for path spaces `ℝ → F`, the one generated by evaluations). -/
def CouplingConverges {E : Type*} [MeasurableSpace E] (conv : (ℕ → E) → E → Prop)
    {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (P : Measure Ω) (X : ℕ → Ω → E)
    {Ω' : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω'] (P' : Measure Ω') (X' : Ω' → E) : Prop :=
  ∃ (Ω₀ : Type) (_ : MeasurableSpace Ω₀) (Q : Measure Ω₀) (Y : ℕ → Ω₀ → E) (Y' : Ω₀ → E),
    IsProbabilityMeasure Q ∧ (∀ n, Measurable (Y n)) ∧ Measurable Y' ∧
    (∀ᶠ n in atTop, Q.map (Y n) = P.map (X n)) ∧ Q.map Y' = P'.map X' ∧
    ∀ᵐ ω ∂Q, conv (fun n => Y n ω) (Y' ω)

end ChenWhitt93.JumpDiffusion


